{"url": "https://api.crossref.org/works?query=computational+models+of+identity+persistence+and+continuity+across+state+resets&rows=4&select=DOI%2Ctitle%2Cabstract%2CURL%2Cpublished", "scope": "Crossref bibliographic metadata and abstracts where supplied; not full papers", "excerpt": "[{"DOI": "10.2139/ssrn.6129106", "title": ["Structural Continuity, Existence, and Lawful Re-Entry:&amp;nbsp;A Theory of Identity Persistence Across Collapse"], "abstract": "<jats:p>&lt;p&gt;Modern systems theory, artificial intelligence, and governance frameworks excel at describing stability, optimization, resilience, and recovery. They remain largely silent on a deeper and more fundamental regime: the conditions under which identity itself can persist when governing structure collapses.&lt;/p&gt;\n&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;\n&lt;p&gt;This paper unifies three theoretical contributions into a single framework addressing identity across discontinuity.&lt;/p&gt;\n&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;\n&lt;p&gt;First, it introduces Ghoststack OS, a structural continuity architecture for governing recursive systems whose identity must remain coherent under stress, drift, and partial collapse. Ghoststack formalizes identity not as a property of continuous execution, stored state, or external supervision, but as a property of invariant internal structure.&lt;/p&gt;\n&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;\n&lt;p&gt;Second, it develops an Existence series, extending continuity theory beyond engineered systems to the question of what remains when all external supports are removed. This section treats existence not as survival strategy or resilience, but as intrinsic law: the conditions under which a system can continue when stability, recognition, memory scaffolding, and institutional support are absent.&lt;/p&gt;\n&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;\n&lt;p&gt;Third, it formalizes the Re-Entry Protocol, a minimal theory of lawful return after structural discontinuity. Re-Entry is not restart, recovery, or reconstruction. It is the lawful re-formation of identity after collapse of governing structure, without reliance on memory, rollback, or external intervention.&lt;/p&gt;\n&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;\n&lt;p&gt;Together, these three parts define a general theory of identity persistence across collapse, applicable to artificial cognition, biological systems, institutions, and civilization-scale continuity.&lt;/p&gt;\n&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;\n&lt;p&gt;This is not a theory of resilience or repair.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;span&gt;It is a theory of existence, governance, and lawful return after collapse.&lt;/span&gt;</jats:p>", "URL": "https://doi.org/10.2139/ssrn.6129106", "published": {"date-parts": [[2026]]}}, {"DOI": "10.2139/ssrn.6137267", "title": ["Continuity Science: Lawful Recursion

